Z||000001||Borgio||11-02-2001||01:29 AM||||My brother has had two "iron dukes" and he didn't need nos to blow them! Its sad, I know. I don't know the answer to your question really. But if I were you I would do a V6 conversion first anyway. A 3.4 infact, thats what I'm gonna do, mine is a 6 already though. Then you will get even more power anyway. Nos isn't extremely cheap. But doing an engine swap isn't incredibly easy or cheap either. Maybe nos is exactly what you're looking for, but I'll let someone who actually knows what they are talking about answer that.<P>------------------<BR>Jake||||reg||1
Z||000002||fotofrank||11-02-2001||01:19 PM||||BOOM ---- the stroke is too long. Also the compression ratio is too high. About 8.5:1 is a good ratio for a turbo or suppercharger.<P>------------------<BR>Peace and Happiness<BR>Frank and his fotoflash, an '84 SE 2M4 <A HREF="http://www.homestead.com/fotofrank/foto1.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.homestead.com/fotofrank/foto1.html</A> <p>[This message has been edited by fotofrank (edited 11-02-2001).]||||reg||4
Z||000003||Pheagey||11-02-2001||02:03 PM||||   I'm sayin' go with the turbo charger unless you are also rebuilding the engine w/ high performance parts. NOS will...well...make a stock 4c a moving gernade.<BR>  I have a 4 myself and am looking at anything to bust HP w/ putting to much train on the engine so I feel ur pain.<P>------------------<BR>"There is no replacement for displacement.Period"<BR>    -Mr.Frosty<P>'88 coupe, 5 speed<BR> <A HREF="Http://www.LithiumEffects.com/car.html" TARGET=_blank>Http://www.LithiumEffects.com/car.html</A> <P>Killed: '97 Civic LX Auto,<BR>Killed by: '93 Civic EX 5 speed,<p>[This message has been edited by Pheagey (edited 11-02-2001).]||||reg||2
